<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>It is rarely too late to hire a public adjuster while a claim is still open, but each stage narrows your options.</li>
<li>The earlier the better: before the insurer inspects is ideal, but many people get help after a low offer or a denial.</li>
<li>Deadlines matter more than timing. TWIA claims generally must be filed within one year of the date of loss, and appraisal and lawsuit deadlines can be much shorter than people expect.</li>
<li>A signed release, a missed appraisal window, or an expired contractual suit deadline can effectively end your options.</li>
<li>Once a lawsuit is likely or filed, you need a Texas attorney. A public adjuster is not a lawyer.</li>
